# Text is normalised to LF in the repository and checked out as LF everywhere,
# rather than left to each machine's core.autocrlf.
#
# Why this file exists: a Windows checkout committed four files as CRLF via PR
# #129. When one side of a merge is CRLF and the other is LF, every line of the
# file differs, so git finds no hunks and reports the whole file as a single
# conflict. Merging #131 hit 15 conflicted files; 4 were pure line-ending noise
# and 2 of those had no real content difference at all once \r was stripped.
# `git -c merge.renormalize=true` is not the escape hatch it looks like — it
# reads its normalisation rules from this file, so with no .gitattributes there
# was nothing for it to act on. See docs/decision-log.md, 2026-09-10.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary artefacts must never be line-ending converted. git's auto-detection
# would very likely classify these correctly on its own via their NUL bytes,
# but the two committed gzip artefacts really do contain CR bytes (916 and
# 4,805 of them), and the .npy files are the golden regression fixtures — a
# normalisation pass that rewrote one of those would corrupt the dataset the
# API serves or the baseline the tests compare against, silently, rather than
# failing loudly. Too cheap to leave to a heuristic. The image and font
# extensions are pre-emptive; none are tracked yet.
*.gz    binary
*.npy   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
